Track 2: ¿Dónde está el malabarista?
- Story & Context: Old Man Marceau sat in the dressing room clutching a box of moth-eaten juggling pins, wondering if he had truly lost his balance or if the world had simply bent its geometry to accommodate the new tempo. He heard the muffled thud of the drums through the canvas walls, tasting the metallic copper of a nosebleed as he confessed, "I sold the balance for a moment of flight," while staring at his shaking, uncoordinated hands.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A close-up of a weather-beaten, greasepaint-smeared face reflected in a shattered, kaleidoscopic circus mirror, dimly lit by a flickering golden light.

Track 3: The Weaver’s Last Fault
- Story & Context: Years after the truce, the tent that hosted the wedding has become a pilgrimage site for dancers seeking to understand why the ground still vibrates at the exact frequency of a snapping bungee cord. Visitors describe the strange, lingering scent of burnt silk that marks the spot, echoing the final, mythic lyric inscribed on the collapsed center pole: "Everything is mending, nothing is ever whole."
